    <node><name>1</name><description>Both contain a and b</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans5</sans><tans>"both"</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt5-2-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Every vector in this question is written with \(\mathbf{a}\) and \(\mathbf{b}\), so that alone cannot be the reason. Ask when \(p\mathbf{a} + q\mathbf{b}\) could equal \(r\mathbf{a} + s\mathbf{b}\) with different coefficients: what would have to be true of \(\mathbf{a}\) and \(\mathbf{b}\)?</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt5-2-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[[[if test="is(ch=1)"]]<p>The lengths of \(\mathbf{a}\) and \(\mathbf{b}\) do not matter: the argument works for a triangle of any shape. Think about directions: if \(\mathbf{b}\) were a multiple of \(\mathbf{a}\), could one vector be written with different pairs of coefficients?</p>[[/if]][[if test="is(ch=2)"]]<p>If every vector were a multiple of \(\mathbf{a}\), then \(\mathbf{b}\) would be too, and O, A and B would lie on one line: there would be no triangle. A vector such as \(2\mathbf{a}\) could then be written in many ways, so coefficients could not be equated. What must be true of \(\mathbf{a}\) and \(\mathbf{b}\) to rule that out?</p>[[/if]]]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
